thousands of seriously ill coronavirus  patients  have now been released from u.s  hospitals all carry permanent scars  but only seven in the u.s were saved  by a rare double lung transplant the  procedure is complex and is only  performed on people with terminal  irreversible damage after the virus  ravaged their lungs  cbs news chief medical correspondent dr  john lepuk spoke exclusively  with one of these survivors  in mid-march the early days of the  pandemic in the u.s  coronavirus was not on brian cunes's  mind  i started just a very little about it  sure enough so the next day i heard  about  next it days because i got hit with it  the 62 year old was working at his  suburban chicago auto shop  when he hit a wall i just got back from  lunch and i got sick  boom i was done he would shake so bad  like a jackhammer that was like a shiver  but i mean a  violently jerking shake cune's wife  nancy rushed him to the hospital i don't  remember anything for like  a month and a half after that the first  time i woke up in the hospital  i couldn't breathe his lungs were  severely damaged and unable to supply  enough oxygen to his body  even with a ventilator so doctors  started ecmo  a life support treatment that uses a  machine outside the body to add oxygen  to blood  he was on it for a hundred days i could  feel it my lung wasn't working  it's not moving i breathe alone in the  hospital  he prepared his wife daughters and  grandchildren for the worst  i said don't expect me to come home my  lungs are done  i ain't coming home this is it yeah  love you goodbye and then finally  what happened they said there's nothing  else we can do  it's either get a transplant or die like  took a chance  and those guys were relentless they  wouldn't give up  those guys were dr ankit bharat and his  transplant team at northwestern medicine  this was the treatment of last resort  for him we told him like i cannot  you know say with certainty that he will  get through this but we have done it  once before  just a month earlier dr bharat's team  performed the first successful double  lung transplant on a covet patient in  the u.s  she was 28 years old now it was kunz's  turn  when you opened up his chest in the  operating room what did his lungs look  like  in patients who had this covalent 19  lung injury and lung failure they  developed these punched out holes  throughout the lungs and brian he had  big time big cavities  the lung was stuck to the heart to the  diaphragm to the test wall  the day after the 10 hour surgery  doctors took brian off the ventilator to  test out his new lungs  and of course the first thing i do is go  wow  because i could breathe again what was  that like  it was something that was good you could  feel air moving in  yeah i'm moving out is that okay  okay getting tired cunes has weeks of  rehabilitation before he can go home  he's lost weight and muscle tone but  gained a new appreciation for the  miracle of a simple breath of air  because like for a couple days i i was  breathing  non-stop 24 7  like that so when it's doing itself  you don't even know it that's great i  suspect you will never  take breathing for granted again  i won't dr bharat says this rare  treatment may be expanded to help a  relatively  small number of other severely ill  covert 19 patients  meanwhile after working for years as a  self-employed mechanic  cuets has now retired and told me i got  a little camper  maybe i'll go see the usa  [Laughter]  wow john what a story and those doctors  are miracle workers thank you very much
 

